    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#head-to-head" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. The Comparison Table</a>
          <a href="#what-is-epoxy" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. What Epoxy Actually Is</a>
          <a href="#what-is-polyurea" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. What Polyurea Actually Is</a>
          <a href="#hybrid" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. The Hybrid System</a>
          <a href="#choosing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Which Should You Choose?</a>
          <a href="#cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. What Each Costs</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          These two coatings fail in opposite ways. Epoxy is hard, rigid and cheap, and it cracks when the slab moves or ambers when the sun hits it. Polyurea is flexible, UV-stable and forgiving, and it cures so fast that an amateur cannot keep a wet edge. That trade — durability against workability — is the whole comparison.
        </p>

        <div id="head-to-head" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Epoxy vs Polyurea at a Glance
          </h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Epoxy</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Polyurea</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Chemistry</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rigid thermoset, resin plus hardener</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flexible elastomer, rapid-reacting</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Working time</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20-40 minutes typical</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often under 10 minutes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Walk-on time</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">18-24 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Frequently 2-6 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Full cure</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7 days and up</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-2 days</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">UV stability</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Poor; ambers in 2-4 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Excellent; does not yellow</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Flexibility</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rigid; cracks if the slab moves</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Elastic; bridges hairline movement</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cold weather application</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Narrow window, needs warmth</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wide temperature tolerance</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Abrasion resistance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Very good</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Excellent</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">DIY friendly</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, the standard kit choice</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rarely; pot life defeats amateurs</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Relative cost</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lower</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Substantially higher</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Read that table and one thing should jump out: polyurea wins nearly every technical row, and epoxy wins the two rows that decide most actual purchases — cost and whether you can apply it yourself. That is not a coincidence. Polyurea is the better material and epoxy is the more accessible one.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Honest One-Line Answer</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Hiring a contractor: polyurea. Doing it yourself: epoxy.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Polyurea’s short pot life is not a minor inconvenience — it is the reason the product is effectively professional-only. A crew with the right equipment and two people working a wet edge gets a superb floor. One person with a roller gets lap marks and a partially cured pail.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="what-is-epoxy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Epoxy Actually Is and Where It Wins
          </h2>

          <p>
            Garage floor epoxy is a two-part thermoset: a resin and a hardener that react when mixed and cure into a hard, rigid plastic bonded to the concrete. It has been the default garage floor coating for decades because it is genuinely good at the core job — it is hard, it resists abrasion and most household chemicals, it is easy to clean, and it transforms a dusty grey slab into something that looks finished.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Epoxy Genuinely Wins</h3>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Cost. It is materially cheaper than polyurea per square foot, both in materials and installed.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Pot life. Twenty to forty minutes of working time is enough for one person with a roller to keep a wet edge.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Availability. Consumer kits are stocked everywhere, in a wide range of quality levels.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Build. High-solids epoxy lays down a thick film per coat, which is what fills minor surface imperfections.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Familiarity. Every installer knows it, and troubleshooting information is abundant.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Epoxy’s Two Real Weaknesses</h3>

          <p>
            The first is ultraviolet light. Standard epoxy is not UV stable, and a floor that gets direct sun through an open garage door or a window will develop a yellow cast, often within two to four years. It is a cosmetic failure rather than a structural one, but it is visible and irreversible. The standard fix is a UV-stable topcoat, which is frequently polyaspartic or polyurea — the products epoxy is being compared against.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second is rigidity. Cured epoxy is hard and comparatively brittle. When the concrete beneath it moves — thermal cycling, a hairline crack opening, minor settlement — the coating has little ability to accommodate that movement and can crack or delaminate along the line. On a sound, stable slab this rarely matters. On a slab with a history of movement, it matters a great deal.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-is-polyurea" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Polyurea Actually Is and Where It Wins
          </h2>

          <p>
            Polyurea is an elastomeric coating formed by a very fast reaction between an isocyanate and an amine resin. The defining property is speed: the reaction is measured in minutes rather than hours, which is why professional crews can grind, coat and hand a garage back to a homeowner within a day.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second defining property is elasticity. Where epoxy cures rigid, polyurea cures flexible, and that flexibility lets it move with a slab rather than fracturing when the slab moves. It bridges hairline cracks that would telegraph straight through an epoxy film, and it tolerates the thermal cycling of an unheated garage far better.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Polyurea Genuinely Wins</h3>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>UV stability. It does not amber or yellow in sunlight, so a sunlit garage keeps its colour.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Speed of return to service. Walk-on in hours rather than a day, full use in a day or two.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Temperature tolerance. It can be applied across a much wider range, including cold conditions that stop epoxy.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Flexibility. It accommodates slab movement and bridges hairline cracks.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Abrasion and impact resistance. It is tougher than epoxy against dropped tools and dragged equipment.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Hot tire pickup. Its resistance to softening under hot tyres is better than most epoxies.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Polyurea’s Two Real Weaknesses</h3>

          <p>
            Cost is the obvious one — it is substantially more expensive than epoxy in both material and installed price, and for a plain storage garage that premium can be hard to justify.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other is that same defining speed, viewed from the applicator’s side. A pot life that can be under ten minutes means there is no margin for error. Mix a full batch and hesitate, and it cures in the pail. Work alone across a large floor and you cannot maintain a wet edge, which leaves permanent lap lines. This is why polyurea is overwhelmingly a professionally installed product and why consumer polyurea kits are comparatively rare.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hybrid" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The System Most Good Installers Actually Sell
          </h2>

          <p>
            The framing of epoxy versus polyurea is slightly artificial, because the best-performing garage floors generally use both. The industry-standard premium system is a layered one, and understanding it clarifies what you are being quoted.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">1</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Mechanical surface preparation</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Diamond grinding or shot blasting to open the concrete pores. This is the single biggest determinant of how long any coating lasts, and it is what separates a professional install from a kit.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">2</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Crack and joint repair</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Cracks are routed, filled and, where appropriate, the control joints are honoured rather than coated over.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">3</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Primer or base coat</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Often an epoxy, chosen precisely because it builds film thickness economically and bonds superbly to properly prepared concrete.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">4</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Decorative flake broadcast</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Vinyl flake broadcast to refusal into the wet base coat, which provides the finished appearance and adds texture.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">5</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Polyurea or polyaspartic topcoat</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The wear surface. This is where UV stability, chemical resistance and abrasion resistance actually come from.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            That structure explains the economics neatly. Epoxy is used where its strengths lie — cheap film build and excellent adhesion to prepared concrete — and the expensive, fast-curing, UV-stable material is reserved for the thin top layer where those properties are actually needed. A quote describing an epoxy base with a polyurea or polyaspartic topcoat is not a compromise; it is the premium specification.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">What This Means When Reading a Quote</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Ask what the topcoat is, not just what the system is called.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A floor sold as a polyurea system may be an epoxy base with a polyurea wear layer, which is genuinely excellent. A floor sold as epoxy with no topcoat mentioned is a different product with a shorter life and a yellowing risk. The topcoat is where most of the performance and most of the cost sits.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which One Should You Actually Choose?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Work through these in order and the decision usually makes itself.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Choose Epoxy If</h3>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>You are applying it yourself and want a realistic chance of a good result.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>The budget is the primary constraint and the garage is mainly for storage and parking.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>The garage gets little or no direct sunlight, so ambering is not a concern.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>The slab is sound and stable, with no history of cracking or movement.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>You are comfortable with a floor that may need refreshing in several years rather than lasting decades.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Choose Polyurea If</h3>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>You are hiring a professional installer anyway, so pot life is their problem rather than yours.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>The garage gets direct sun and you care about the colour holding.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>You use the space as a workshop or gym, where impact and abrasion resistance matter.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>The slab has hairline cracking or the garage is unheated and cycles through big temperature swings.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>You need the garage back in service quickly rather than losing it for a week.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            For most homeowners the honest summary is this: if you are hiring out the work, the incremental cost of a polyurea or polyaspartic topcoat over a bare epoxy system is one of the better upgrades available, because it addresses epoxy’s two genuine weaknesses directly. If you are doing the work yourself, buy the best high-solids epoxy kit you can afford, prepare the slab properly, and accept that preparation rather than product will determine how long it lasts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Each One Actually Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Precise pricing moves with region, slab condition and how much preparation is needed, but the relative structure is stable and worth understanding before you read a quote.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">System</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Relative Cost</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Where the Money Goes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">DIY water-based epoxy kit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lowest</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Almost entirely materials</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">DIY high-solids epoxy plus topcoat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low-moderate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Materials, plus grinder rental</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Professional epoxy only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly half is preparation labour</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Professional epoxy with polyurea topcoat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Highest</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Preparation, plus premium topcoat material</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The point most quotes obscure is that a large share of a professional installation is the grinding, not the coating. That is why a polyurea system is not proportionally more expensive than an epoxy one — you are paying the same preparation cost either way and the difference is confined to the topcoat material. Upgrading the topcoat on a job you are already paying to have prepared is therefore much better value than the headline material prices suggest.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Thinking in Cost Per Year</h3>

          <p>
            A budget kit lasting three to five years, redone repeatedly, frequently costs more over two decades than a single professional installation lasting fifteen to twenty-five — and each redo means emptying the garage and losing it for a week. Set against that, the premium system often wins on both money and disruption. The calculation changes if you may move within a few years, where a high-solids DIY epoxy is the sensible middle path.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            7. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Polyurea Kit for a Solo DIY Install</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Polyurea’s pot life can be under ten minutes, which leaves no margin to mix, pour, spread and maintain a wet edge across a garage floor alone. The result is lap lines where sections cured before the next pass reached them, and often a partly cured pail. If you are working solo, epoxy’s twenty to forty minute window is the realistic choice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Standard Epoxy in a Sunlit Garage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Standard epoxy is not UV stable and will develop a yellow cast, often within two to four years, wherever direct sunlight falls through an open door or a window. The colour change is permanent and cannot be polished out. If the space gets real sun, specify a UV-stable topcoat over the epoxy rather than leaving it exposed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Mechanical Preparation Because the Product Is Premium</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Neither coating bonds to a sealed, polished or contaminated slab, and no amount of money spent on the coating compensates. Diamond grinding or shot blasting to open the pores is the single largest factor in how long the floor lasts. A well-prepared epoxy floor outlives a badly prepared polyurea one every time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Quotes Without Comparing Topcoats</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Two quotes described as garage floor coating systems can differ enormously depending on whether there is a polyurea or polyaspartic wear layer on top and how thick it is. Ask specifically what the topcoat is, how many coats, and what the total system thickness is, rather than comparing headline prices.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating Over Active Moisture</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A slab pushing moisture vapour up from below will delaminate any film coating, epoxy or polyurea alike, regardless of preparation quality. Test for moisture before committing — a taped plastic sheet left down overnight is the simple version — and address the source or use a moisture-tolerant system rather than hoping.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is polyurea better than epoxy for a garage floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Technically yes on most measures — polyurea is UV stable, flexible enough to bridge hairline cracks, more abrasion resistant and returns the garage to service in hours rather than days. Epoxy wins on cost and on pot life, which is what makes it the practical choice for DIY installation. For a professional install, polyurea or a polyurea topcoat is the better floor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you apply polyurea garage floor coating yourself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is difficult and rarely advisable. Polyurea can have a pot life under ten minutes, which leaves no margin to mix, spread and maintain a wet edge across a whole garage single-handed. Lap lines and cured product in the pail are the usual outcomes. Epoxy’s twenty to forty minute working window is far more forgiving for a solo installer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does polyurea yellow like epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Standard epoxy is not UV stable and typically develops a yellow cast within two to four years where direct sun reaches it. Polyurea is UV stable and holds its colour, which is exactly why it is so commonly used as the topcoat over an epoxy base in premium garage floor systems.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does polyurea take to cure compared to epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Polyurea is dramatically faster. It is often walk-on within a few hours and fully cured in a day or two, against roughly 18 to 24 hours for walk-on and seven days or more for full chemical cure with epoxy. That speed is why professional crews can grind and coat a garage in a single day.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is polyurea worth the extra cost over epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you are hiring an installer, generally yes — it addresses epoxy’s two real weaknesses, UV ambering and rigidity, and adds abrasion resistance and a much faster return to service. For a plain storage garage with no sunlight and a tight budget, a well-prepared high-solids epoxy is a sound choice and the premium is harder to justify.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between polyurea and polyaspartic?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Polyaspartic is a subset of polyurea chemistry, formulated with a slower reaction so it has a longer, more workable pot life. In practice the terms are often used interchangeably in the garage floor trade, and both are used as UV-stable topcoats over an epoxy base. Polyaspartic is generally the more forgiving of the two to apply.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

