        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Polyurea is the better material and epoxy is the more practical one, and which of those matters more depends entirely on who is holding the roller. Polyurea is UV stable, flexible, tougher and back in service in hours. Epoxy is cheaper, stocked everywhere, and has a pot life a single person can actually work with.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If you are hiring a contractor, the question is not really epoxy or polyurea — it is what the topcoat is. The premium specification most good installers sell is an epoxy base coat for economical film build and adhesion, with a polyurea or polyaspartic wear layer on top providing the UV stability and abrasion resistance. That is where the performance and most of the cost sit, so ask about it specifically when comparing quotes.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If you are doing this yourself, buy epoxy, buy the best high-solids kit your budget allows, and then spend your effort on the part that actually decides the outcome: mechanical preparation of the slab. A properly ground and profiled floor coated in mid-range epoxy will comfortably outlast a premium product rolled onto a slab that was merely swept and acid-etched.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
